Logic :

Same as like solution of Leetcode 46 (Permutations)

solved this problem using backtracking with an early stopping optimization. 
First, create an array containing the numbers from 1 to n. 
then use a boolean visited array to track which numbers have already been included in the current permutation and a temporary list to build the permutation step by step.
At each recursive call, iterate through all the numbers. If a number has not been used,  add it to the current permutation, mark it as visited, and recursively continue building the remaining positions.
When the current permutation reaches length n, it have generated one complete permutation. hence , store a copy of it in the answer list and decrement a global counter, which initially stores the value of k.
To avoid generating unnecessary permutations, check whether the counter has reached zero at the beginning of every recursive call.
Once k permutations have been generated, I immediately stop further recursion and return. This optimization prevents exploring the remaining permutation tree after finding the required number of permutations.
Finally, since it stopped exactly after generating the kth permutation, the last permutation stored in the answer list is the required answer. 
convert that permutation into a string and return it.


Code :

class Solution {
    int g=0;
    public String getPermutation(int n, int k) {
        int[] nums=new int[n];
        for(int i=1;i<=n;i++){
            nums[i-1]=i;
        }
        g=k;
        List<List<Integer>> ans=new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> list=new ArrayList<>();
        boolean[] f=new boolean[nums.length];
        permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
        String s="";
        for(int num:ans.get(ans.size()-1)){
            s+=num;
        }
        return s;

    }
    void permutation(int[] nums,boolean[] f,List<Integer> list,List<List<Integer>> ans){
        if(g==0){
            return;
        }
        if(list.size()==nums.length){
            ans.add(new ArrayList<>(list));
            g--;
            return;
        }
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(!f[i]){
                list.add(nums[i]);
                f[i]=true;
                permutation(nums,f,list,ans);
                f[i]=false;
                list.remove(list.size()-1);
            }
        }
    }
}
